                    briefing_text_r1:       `Meridian's CHRO names three competing challenges — <strong>frontline-to-enterprise leadership</strong>, <strong>digital & AI adoption</strong>, and <strong>cross-business-unit collaboration</strong> — and deliberately refuses to pick. Engineer the program backward from the organizational outcome, not forward from content faculty enjoy teaching. Choose ONE center of gravity, name the behavior you intend to change, and state the leading indicator that would prove it at day 90. The trap: chasing all three to please her. At <em>$4,000 per participant spread across three behaviors</em>, depth collapses below the threshold where any behavior can be measured — and the CFO sees nothing move.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the learning experience for transfer`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Compose the delivery model under the <strong>$480,000 cap</strong>, justifying each choice against transfer-of-learning, not appeal. The 70-20-10 logic is unforgiving: learning that sticks is reinforced on the job — through projects, coaching, manager involvement, and spaced nudges — not delivered once in a classroom. Buy a single 2-day event and the <em>forgetting curve eats 70% of it in three weeks</em>, leaving your day-90 behavior index flat. And reserve budget for measurement now: spend the full cap on delivery and you cannot afford to honor Catalyst's promise to prove impact.`,
