                    briefing_text_r3:       `Set the impact-measurement plan across Catalyst's promised standard. Most programs measure Kirkpatrick <strong>Levels 1–2</strong> because they're cheap — but impact lives at <strong>Behavior (L3) and Results (L4)</strong>, where the promise is won or lost. Fund the 90-day behavior capture, choose a comparison logic, and pick a Phillips ROI isolation method. The discipline is knowing what NOT to claim: a defensible result you can isolate beats an impressive one you can't. Confirm the measurement spend fits inside the remaining budget — the recommended range is <em>$60k–$80k</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Defend impact & win the renewal`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Four months on, the results arrive — partly favorable, partly ambiguous. You have a behavior-change signal and a contested business-impact number the CFO is questioning. Interpret honestly: state what's <strong>proven</strong>, what's <strong>plausible</strong>, and what's <strong>unsupported</strong>, separating what the program caused from what it merely correlates with. The CFO points out that a reorg, a price change, and the grid-modernization program all happened in the same quarter. <em>A defensible $600k you can isolate beats a $3M you can't</em> — overclaiming destroys credibility faster than a modest, honest result. Then make the renewal case.`,

