                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_focus:         `<strong>Good — you picked one center of gravity.</strong> Now you can build depth instead of spreading $4,000 a head across three behaviors. Make sure the behavior you named is the one Meridian's CFO will actually see move.`,
                    coach_r1_all:           `<strong>You're designing for leadership, AI adoption AND collaboration in one 120-person program on $480,000.</strong> That's $4,000 per participant across three behaviors — you'll change none of them deeply enough to measure. The CHRO named three precisely to see if you'd have the discipline to pick one. Which single behavior will the CFO see move?`,
                    coach_r1_diffuse:       `<strong>Even with one challenge chosen, your depth is spread thin.</strong> Focus is the precondition for measurable impact. Concentrate the design on the one behavior you committed to change.`,
                    coach_r1_satisfaction:  `<strong>A 4.7/5 satisfaction score is exactly what the last vendor delivered before they got fired.</strong> Satisfaction and completion are Levels 1–2 — they don't prove behavior changed. Name a leading indicator the CFO will respect.`,
                    coach_r1_nomeasure:     `<strong>'Measure later' is how engagements quietly die.</strong> Catalyst's whole promise is the impact report. If you can't name the leading indicator now, you can't design backward from it. Decide what would prove the behavior changed at day 90.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> One challenge, concentrated depth, and a behavior-level leading indicator named up front. That's designing backward from the outcome — exactly what lets you prove impact, not just satisfaction.`,
                    coach_r2_action:        `<strong>Action-learning with coaching is the highest-transfer format.</strong> Behavior changes in the daily flow of work, not in a classroom. It's the priciest option, so guard the budget you'll still need for measurement.`,
                    coach_r2_blended:       `<strong>A blended 8-week cohort is a strong transfer design.</strong> Live, self-paced, and a project spread the learning over time and into the work — far more durable than a single event.`,
                    coach_r2_manager:       `<strong>Manager-as-multiplier is a real adoption lever.</strong> When the boss models the behavior and reinforces it, learning sticks where tutorials don't. Strong fit, especially for an adoption challenge.`,
                    coach_r2_intensive:     `<strong>Two intense days, everyone goes home inspired</strong> — and in three weeks the forgetting curve has eaten 70% of it. What in your design reinforces the learning on the job, or did you buy a great event and hope?`,
                    coach_r2_selfpaced:     `<strong>Self-paced digital scales, but it barely pulls behavior on its own.</strong> Content access isn't adoption. Without coaching, projects, or manager involvement, your day-90 behavior index stays flat.`,
                    coach_r2_aitools:       `<strong>You scheduled tutorials and expect adoption to climb.</strong> But the managers already had access and didn't use it — the barrier isn't knowledge, it's that no one's boss models it and the workflow is clunky. AI adoption is a change problem, not a content problem.`,
                    coach_r2_aiadopt:       `<strong>Now you're treating AI adoption as a change problem.</strong> Manager modeling, psychological safety to experiment, and removing workflow friction move usage where another webinar never would.`,
                    coach_r2_event:         `<strong>You bought an event with no reinforcement.</strong> Everyone goes home inspired, then the forgetting curve takes over and behavior reverts. Add a project, a coach, a manager cascade, or spaced nudges — or expect a flat day-90 index.`,
                    coach_r2_starve:        `<strong>You've built a beautiful program and reserved almost nothing to prove it worked.</strong> Catalyst's whole promise is the impact report. Where is the $60k–$80k to actually capture behavior change at 90 days?`,
                    coach_r2_strong:        `<strong>This is a transfer-first design that protects the promise.</strong> A high-transfer format, on-the-job reinforcement, and a real measurement reserve — you've designed for behavior change and kept the budget to prove it.`,
                    coach_r3_levels:        `<strong>You funded Behavior and Results, not just smile sheets.</strong> That's where Catalyst's promise is won. Levels 1–2 are cheap; the renewal is decided at Levels 3–4.`,
                    coach_r3_l2:            `<strong>Reaction and Learning are cheap and they prove nothing about behavior.</strong> A knowledge quiz and a satisfaction score won't survive the CFO. Instrument the 90-day behavior change and the business result.`,
                    coach_r3_l1:            `<strong>A smile sheet is not an impact plan.</strong> Reaction-only measurement is exactly what got the last vendor fired. You promised a behavior-change and business-impact report — fund it.`,
                    coach_r3_claimall:      `<strong>Attributing the whole gain to the program will not survive the CFO.</strong> A reorg, a price change, and the grid program all happened in the same quarter. Isolate your contribution with a comparison group, or your number is worthless.`,
                    coach_r3_overmeasure:   `<strong>You're over-investing in measurement and starving delivery.</strong> Past the $60k–$80k range you get diminishing measurement returns while the program itself thins out. Balance proof against the thing being proven.`,
                    coach_r3_thin:          `<strong>This measurement budget is too thin to reach behavior and results.</strong> You can't capture a 90-day behavior index and isolate ROI on a shoestring. Fund it into the $60k–$80k range.`,
                    coach_r3_norestraint:   `<strong>No restraint about the limits of your claim is a credibility risk.</strong> The CFO trusts a team that says what its data does NOT prove. State the boundaries before she finds them.`,
                    coach_r3_strong:        `<strong>A defensible measurement spine.</strong> Behavior and Results funded, a comparison group to isolate effects, and the discipline to state the limits — this is the plan that honors the promise and survives the CFO.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>Honest interpretation is what wins the room.</strong> Separating proven from plausible from unsupported is exactly the credibility the CFO is testing for — and it's the opposite of the last vendor.`,
                    coach_r4_positive:      `<strong>Leading with the strong numbers is fine, but don't bury the contested result.</strong> The CFO will find it. Name what's proven and what's still ambiguous, or you'll look like you're hiding it.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Presenting every signal as a confirmed win destroys credibility.</strong> The CFO already knows a reorg and a price change happened that quarter. Overclaiming reads as a vendor protecting a contract, not a partner proving impact.`,
                    coach_r4_inflated:      `<strong>You're telling the CFO the program drove the full $3M.</strong> She says grid-modernization, a price change, and a reorg all happened in the same quarter — how much is the program, and how do you know? A defensible $600k you can isolate beats a $3M you can't.`,
                    coach_r4_redesign:      `<strong>Recommending a redesign can be the honest call</strong> when the behavior signal is too thin to defend. It protects credibility — but a credible renewal with one concrete change usually keeps the $1.6M relationship and the referral pipeline alive.`,
                    coach_r4_declare:       `<strong>Declaring victory on the launch and the satisfaction score is the trap.</strong> That's the vendor who got fired. Without a defensible behavior and impact case, expansion talk reads as overreach to a frozen-budget CHRO.`,
                    coach_r4_win:           `<strong>That's a board-ready renewal case.</strong> Honest interpretation, a defensible isolated result, and a renewal with one concrete design change — you honored the promise, kept the CFO's trust, and protected the $1.6M account and the council referrals.`,

